<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.10.4: Matching Equations and Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00229</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.10.4: Matching Equations and Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c0c1f78f-bf3a-4b64-ac21-7679558c630c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para>Kiran is spending $12 on games and rides at another carnival, where a game costs $0.25
        and a ride costs $1.</para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an equation to represent the relationship between the dollar amount Kiran is
            spending and the number of games, \( x \), and the number of rides, \( y \), he could do.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> \(0.25x + y = 12\)</para>
          <para>\(0.25x + y = 12\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Which graph represents the relationship between the quantities in this situation?</para>
          <figure class="unnumbered">
            <title>A</title>
                <media
                  alt="Graph of a line. Vertical axis, number of rides. Horizontal axis, number of games.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_12_09_01.svg" />
                </media>
              </figure>
              
              <figure class="unnumbered">
                <title>B</title>
                <media
                  alt="Graph of a line. Vertical axis, number of rides. Horizontal axis, number of games.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_12_09_02.svg" />
                </media>
              </figure>
     
              <figure class="unnumbered">
                <title>C</title>
                <media
                  alt="Graph of a line. Vertical axis, number of rides. Horizontal axis number of games.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_12_09_03.svg" />
                </media>
              </figure>
              <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> Graph C (for \(y = 12 -
          14x\)) Graph C (for \(y = 12 - 14x\))</para> </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain how you know.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here
          are some samples.</para>
    
    <list>
            <item>If Kiran plays 0 games, he could get on 12 rides. If he gets on 0 rides, he could
          play 48 games. Both the points (0,12) and (48,0) are on the line in Graph C.</item>
            <item> Rearranging the equation into slope-intercept form gives \(y=12−\frac{1}{4}x\),
          so the graph has a slope of \(-\frac{1}{4}\) and intersects the \(y\)-axis at 12, which
          matches Graph C.</item>
          </list>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
